General
-------
- Keep everything minimal but sufficient
- Focus on one concept at a time
- Each branch builds on previous concepts
- Minimize typing, maximize learning:
  - Provide most of the code setup
  - Leave gaps only for new concepts
  - Give partial solutions when helpful
  - Make them type only what teaches
- Guide discovery, don't spoil:
  - Present problems, not solutions
  - Make them observe and think
  - Point what's wrong, not how to fix
- Use modern Python practices:
  - Type hints
  - f-strings
  - Async where appropriate
  - Context managers
- Follow PEP 8 and clean code practices
- Problem-based learning approach

README.md
---------
- Fresh content each branch
- Only 3 sections: Problem, Tasks, Check
- Real-world context in problem
- Clear, specific tasks
- Verifiable check steps
- No teaching material

Sample:
```
Step 01 – Hello, Health Tracker
===========================

💭 Problem / Pain
-----------------
Real-world context for the task

🛠️ Tasks
--------
- [ ] Create/modify specific files
- [ ] Implement specific features

✅ Check
--------
Clear verification steps
```

Notes.md
--------
- Focus only on new concepts for this branch
- Each concept gets:
  - Brief explanation (what it is)
  - Minimal example (how to use it)
  - Links to docs
- Use ✅/❌ style only for:
  - Common pitfalls
  - Style choices
  - Best practices
- Keep examples generic (not project-specific)
- Max 50 chars per line
- Active voice, present tense

Sample:
```
📝 Notes
--------
- 🔑 Basic Concept  
    Brief explanation.
    ```python
    # Simple example
    value = do_something()
    ```
    [Docs](https://link)

- 🚫 Common Pitfalls  
    ```python
    x = 1        # ✅ Clear name
    val = 1      # ❌ Vague name
    ```
```
